Contact derivative oracle
-------------------------
At default edge_fraction=1e-5, the generic weighted Fourier objective's
gap-opening JAX directional derivative in the exploratory complex profile
was 13.448759506573445. Fourth-order forward differences had errors:
  dimensionless step 2e-6: -4.2978061997231976e-3
  dimensionless step 1e-7: -8.072389216239984e-3
  dimensionless step 1e-8: -3.2746625558424114e-3
  dimensionless step 1e-9: -2.5824320369238762e-5
These values do not satisfy the proposed 3e-6 oracle. Smaller steps enter
subtractive roundoff. The finite-difference tolerance was not enlarged.

An independent lossless contact mean-energy variation instead minimizes
the one-dimensional normal trace energy. It uses the default mesh and
checks JAX's opening derivative against that independently derived form
with a 3e-9 absolute bound, at two nonzero shear values. The separate
positive-gap and contact-preserving affine/material directions use two
central finite-difference steps with a 3e-6 bound.

python -m pytest tests/test_nvf_affine_cell.py -q -k derivative
4 passed, 48 deselected in 27.91s (before two graded-y controls were added).
This certifies neither all contact Fourier opening derivatives nor oblique
optical convergence.

Contact Fourier variation and body factorization follow-up, 2026-09-09
--------------------------------------------------------------------
The preceding contact limitations describe the earlier implementation.
The physical affine cell now writes the vanishing body correction as g*w
and cancels g from the corresponding equations analytically. Periodic
derivative and laminated mean-load identities preserve exact zero terms.
This is a change of unknowns for every valid gap; no gap cutoff, clipping,
regularization, optical tolerance change, or custom derivative is used.

tests/test_nvf_affine_contact.py reconstructs physical integrated flux hats
across material/mesh cuts and evaluates the y basis independently. Separate
trace and first-variation body solves yield every field, sesquilinear Gram,
and material Fourier derivative without production assembly, Fourier
moments, projection, or AD. The geometry helper supplies the finite space.
The oracle checks quadrature 16 against 24 to 3e-10, then checks all nine
JAX derivative components through coefficient cap 2 to 3e-9 per normalized
gap. Four cases include real/complex and mixed materials, both shear signs,
body degrees 1/2/4, gap degrees 1/2/4/6, graded/ungraded y and edge 1e-5.

For rows=2, body=2, gap=2, ungraded y, local flux, edge=1e-5,
epsilon=4+.8j, width=350 and J=[[1,.45],[0,.7]]:
  Largest derivative difference from quadrature-24 oracle:
    earlier coupled solve: 1.14125042e-7
    factored body solve:   7.57592697e-11
  Independent quadrature-24 to quadrature-32 difference: 5.90089184e-11
These are discrepancies in finite-cell differentiation, not continuum
error bounds or independent optical contact derivatives.

python -m pytest tests/test_nvf_affine_contact.py -q --tb=short
4 passed in 59.85s.

